Code (requires ndarray = "0.15" or compatible):

```rust
use ndarray::{ArrayD, Axis, IxDyn, concatenate};

/// Remove every `k`-th element along `axis` from `arr`.
/// - `k` must be >= 1.
/// - `axis` is 0-based and must be < arr.ndim().
/// - Semantics: remove indices i where (i+1) % k == 0 (i.e. every k-th element counting from 1).
///
/// Returns a new ArrayD with the same number of dimensions and with the specified axis shortened.
///
/// Example:
/// let a = ndarray::Array::from_shape_vec((2, 7),
///     (0..14).collect()).unwrap().into_dyn();
/// let b = delete_every_kth_along(&a, 3, 1).unwrap();
/// // b has shape (2, 5) because every 3rd element along axis 1 was removed.
pub fn delete_every_kth_along<T: Clone>(
    arr: &ArrayD<T>,
    k: usize,
    axis: usize,
) -> Result<ArrayD<T>, String> {
    if k == 0 {
        return Err("k must be >= 1".into());
    }
    if axis >= arr.ndim() {
        return Err("axis out of bounds".into());
    }

    let axis_len = arr.shape()[axis];
    // Collect views of the slices we want to keep
    let mut kept_views: Vec<_> = Vec::new();
    for i in 0..axis_len {
        if (i + 1) % k != 0 {
            // index_axis borrows from arr; then into_dyn makes it ArrayViewD<'_, T>
            kept_views.push(arr.index_axis(Axis(axis), i).into_dyn());
        }
    }

    // If nothing is kept along that axis, return an empty array with axis length 0.
    if kept_views.is_empty() {
        let mut new_shape = arr.shape().to_vec();
        new_shape[axis] = 0;
        return ArrayD::from_shape_vec(IxDyn(&new_shape), Vec::new())
            .map_err(|e| e.to_string());
    }

    // concatenate expects a slice of ArrayViewD; kept_views contains ArrayViewD already
    concatenate(Axis(axis), &kept_views[..]).map_err(|e| e.to_string())
}
```

Notes:
- The function is generic over element type T but requires Clone only because concatenate returns owned arrays; the code itself uses ArrayView slices, so Clone is not heavily used here but left for flexibility. If you prefer to restrict T further (e.g., Copy) you can.
- If the result has zero length along the chosen axis (all elements were removed), an ArrayD with that axis length 0 is returned.
- If you want a different interpretation of "every var0-th element" (e.g., 0-based every k-th), tell me and I can adjust.

