    // Use order-statistics selection to bring the top `k` elements (by key) into the first `k` slots.
    // We want the largest keys, so we use Reverse(key) to make larger keys compare as "smaller".
    indices.select_nth_unstable_by_key(k - 1, |&i| Reverse(key(&lst[i])));

    // Take the slice of the top k indices and sort them by descending key so output is ordered.
    let mut top = indices[..k].to_vec();
    top.sort_unstable_by_key(|&i| Reverse(key(&lst[i])));

    top
}
```

Notes and tips:
- The function is generic; the key must implement Ord. For integer types or any Ord keys this works directly.
- If your var0 is a floating-point (f32/f64), floating-point types do not implement Ord because of NaN. For f64 you can wrap into an ordered type (for example using the ordered-float crate's OrderedFloat), or convert using a bitwise transform to get a total ordering. Example with ordered-float:
  - add `ordered-float = "2.8"` to Cargo.toml and call `|it: &Item| OrderedFloat(it.var0)` as the key.
- Complexity: selecting the top k is expected O(len) time and O(len) memory for index vector (no cloning of whole items).

