Below is a self-contained Rust function using arrow-rs that removes all rows from a RecordBatch where the value in a given string (Utf8) column equals a given string, then returns a new RecordBatch with rows re-indexed (an "index" Int64 column added at the front, values 0..n-1). It uses the compute::filter kernel to filter each column with a boolean mask that keeps rows where the column value is not equal to the provided value.

Notes:
- This implementation assumes the column var0 is of Utf8 (String) type. It can be adapted for other types by changing the downcast and comparison logic.
- If the column contains nulls, nulls are treated as "not equal" to var1 and are retained.
- If the original table already has a column named "index", the new "index" column will collide — you can rename as desired.

